# Desktop entry for satl-term, the GTK4/VTE window.
#
# The file name is the GApplication id that window.cpp registers, and it has to
# be: GTK puts that id on the toplevel -- as the Wayland app_id, and as the
# X11 WM_CLASS instance because window.cpp sets prgname to it -- and a desktop
# shell then looks for <that id>.desktop. Named satl-term.desktop the window
# arrives associated with no launcher at all: no icon in the switcher, nothing
# to pin. StartupWMClass says the same thing a second time for shells that
# match on WM_CLASS rather than on the file name.
#
# Only satl-term gets an entry. The interpreter is a command-line program with
# no window of its own, and a launcher entry for it would open something the
# user cannot see.
#
# Exec is the bare command name rather than an absolute path, because the prefix
# is not known when this file is written and a hard-coded /usr/local would be
# wrong in every distribution package. TryExec makes the entry hide itself if
# the binary is not on PATH, which is the correct behaviour for a menu.
#
# %f is honest only because window.cpp passes G_APPLICATION_NON_UNIQUE: under
# the single-instance default a second launch handed its argument to the
# already-running process, which read its own empty copy of the variable and
# opened a plain REPL, so the file the user asked for was dropped.
#
# Icon is the application id rather than a file path, because that is how the
# icon theme spec is looked up: the shell searches every theme directory for
# <name> with any extension it understands, so the id names the artwork we
# install under share/icons/hicolor/. It matches the file name and
# StartupWMClass for the same reason all three agree -- a shell that finds the
# launcher by any one of them finds the icon too.
#
# MimeType is what makes this entry the handler for a .satl file: the type is
# declared in dist/application-x-satellite.xml, and a file manager offers the
# programs whose entries name it here. Without this line the type is still
# recognised and still gets its own icon, but opening a file has nothing to
# open it with.
#
# %f in Exec above is what carries the file through, and is already correct for
# this: it is a single-file placeholder, which matches a handler launched once
# per file. It is NOT upgraded to %F -- window.cpp opens one window on one
# path, so a multi-file selection has to arrive as several launches rather than
# as one launch that would silently drop every path but the first.
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Version=1.0
Name=Satellite
GenericName=Satellite Terminal
Comment=Interactive terminal for the satellite programming language
Exec=satl-term %f
TryExec=satl-term
Icon=org.satellite.terminal
Terminal=false
Categories=Development;IDE;
Keywords=satellite;interpreter;repl;language;programming;
MimeType=application/x-satellite;
StartupNotify=true
StartupWMClass=org.satellite.terminal
